<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leo Sgouros</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1208</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leo Sgouros was a Byzantine Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and Corinth in the years around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>. Taking advantage of weakening imperial authority, he extended his power into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. He was the chief opponent of the Frankish conquerors of southern Greece and held out in the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a> until his death around 1208.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leo-sgouros.jpg" alt="Leo Sgouros" width="320" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArisMethymna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leo_Sgouros_seal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leo Sgouros, Latinized as Leo Sgurus, was a scion of the magnate Sgouros family. Around 1198 he succeeded his father, Theodore Sgouros, as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a>, one of the districts known as oria that collected taxes and provided ships for the Byzantine navy. In about 1201/1202, when a rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and Macedonia led by Manuel Kamytzes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobromir-chrysos">Dobromir Chrysos</a> cut southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> off from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, he made himself an independent ruler and captured the citadels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and Corinth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sgouros was profoundly hostile to the Church: he imprisoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplion</a>, and the bishop of Corinth, invited to dinner, was blinded and thrown to his death from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a>. Michael Choniates, the bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, recounts that he beat a young hostage to death for dropping a glass. The megas doux Michael Stryphnos, sent against him, spent the winter of 1201–1202 in Athens without checking him, and Sgouros raided Athens with the help of the islanders of Salamis and Aigina.</p><p>In 1203 his men took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, but its people, led by Choniates, held the Acropolis against a heavy bombardment. Leaving it under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, Sgouros burned the town, stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. Near Larissa he met the deposed emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-angelos">Alexios III Angelos</a>, who had fled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>; in return for protection he received the hand of Alexios's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eudokia-angelina">Eudokia Angelina</a> and the title of despotes. The medievalist John Van Antwerp Fine judged that his state had every chance of becoming a lasting affair.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In autumn 1204 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a> marched south. Sgouros withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a>, where Boniface's second assault broke through, and by spring 1205 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> held the countryside of the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. Blockaded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a>, Sgouros resisted with sorties while the Franks built forts on the hill of Pendeskouphi and on the eastern approaches. According to legend he despaired in 1208 and jumped off the cliffs on his horse. A certain Theodore continued the resistance until the citadel fell in 1210, removing one of the last centres of opposition to the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leo Sgouros” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Leo Sgouros</b> held Acrocorinth until his death in 1208, Corinth fell in 1210 and Argos and Nauplia in 1212, while Venice kept the ports of Modon and Coron in the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of <b>Leo Sgouros</b>, his last strongholds, Argos and Nauplia, fell to the Franks in 1212.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-argos-and-nauplia">Lordship of Argos and Nauplia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Leo Sgouros</b> inherited power as archon of Nauplia from his father around 1198, as Byzantine authority weakened.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-leo-sgouros">Lordship of Leo Sgouros</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Argolida Regional Unit, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.67,22.83&amp;year=1198" aria-label="Argolida Regional Unit on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5120 809 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leo Sgouros"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5570 1079h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5570 1079h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.67,22.83&amp;year=1198">What was here in 1198? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
